# Architecture

Rocci is a workspace of narrow crates rather than one opaque framework. Each
layer owns one kind of transformation and hands explicit artifacts to the next.

## From source to application

```text
.rocci (Templates / Apps)        .rocdown (Documents / Sites)       knowledge (OKF)
        │                                    │                            │
        ▼                                    ▼                            ▼
  rocci-template                       rocci-rocdown                     okf
(parse/lower to Roc)                 (catalog + article HTML)     (portable engine)
        │                                    │                            │
        ▼                                    ▼                            ▼
    rocci-cli                        rocci-rocdown-cli                rocci-okf
 (Roc compile & run)               (static site / doc run)        (review & live reload)
        │                                    │                            │
        └───────────────────┬────────────────┘                            │
                            ▼                                             │
                      rocci-desktop ──────────────────────────────────────┘
                   (preview window host)
```

The template and parsing crates do not invoke Roc, start a server, or open a window. Those
effects belong to the CLI and host layers. The native shell those CLIs open is the
**preview window**: preview chrome is the host-injected navigation overlay; the
webview is the page surface inside it. A Dev control in that overlay can open a
**dev panel** — a preview-origin Rocci inspector, not overlay HTML.

That split follows Roc's own pure-application and effect-providing-platform
model. Read [Why Roc fits Rocci](/docs/concepts/why-roc/) for the effect, capability,
hosting, and execution-mode rationale.

## Workspace responsibilities

| Crate | Responsibility |
| --- | --- |
| `rocci-template` | Parse `.rocci`, lower template regions, preserve source maps |
| `rocci-core` | Configuration (`rocci.toml`) and shared runtime contracts |
| `rocci-desktop` | Native preview window and webview host backed by Tao and Wry |
| `rocci-cli` | `rocci` CLI for `.rocci` applications, dev server, and macOS bundling |
| `rocci-ui` | Domain-neutral view records, presentation components, and base design tokens |
| `rocci-rocdown` | Parse `.rocdown`, build static catalog, render article HTML, and plan site output |
| `rocci-rocdown-cli` | `rocdown` CLI for single documents and multi-page static documentation sites |
| `rocci-theme` | Resolve built-in and local document CSS themes |
| `okf` | Portable, UI-neutral engine for Open Knowledge Format parsing, validation, search, and benchmarks |
| `rocci-okf` | `rocci-okf` CLI and live reload review server for knowledge bundles |
| `rocci-lsp` | Language server for `.rocci` and `.rocdown` (diagnostics, symbols, hover, semantic tokens) |
| `rocci-highlight` | Pinned Tree-sitter highlighter library for Roc, HTML, and CSS |

## Why templates lower to Roc

Rocci is syntax, not a parallel component runtime. Lowering to a small `Html`
constructor interface has useful consequences:

- ordinary Roc types and functions remain the source of truth;
- generated code can be inspected;
- Roc compiler errors can be mapped back to source spans;
- hosts can supply different compatible `Html` implementations;
- a component remains a Roc function rather than an object in a runtime registry.

## Why Rocdown keeps prose in Rust

The first prototype emitted every Markdown node as Roc source. That made
Roc compile time grow with prose, duplicated graph logic needed by the LSP, and
turned routing into missing-standard-library work.

The current split is intentional:

| Concern | Owner |
| --- | --- |
| Parse and catalog documentation | Rust |
| Render static Markdown body | Rust |
| Render navigation and site chrome | Rocci, evaluated by Roc |
| Compile authored dynamic islands | Roc/Rocci, only where used |
| Commit the output tree | Rust |

Content edits therefore do not become large generated Roc modules. The visible
theme remains a real Rocci program.

## The web-platform boundary

The runtime communicates over local HTTP. GET handlers produce documents;
mutation handlers can produce Datastar SSE patches. This keeps the interface
inspectable and gives the preview window and a normal browser the same backend.

Read [the rendering model](/docs/concepts/rendering-model/) for the request and
response semantics.
